<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Netilas Yadayim L'Seudah U'Betzi'as HaPas 6</div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Based on <span class="v">"Ki Merachamam Yenahaigem"</span> <span class="sr">(<span class="lr">LM II, 7</span>)</span>. Already partially explained in Halacha 3.<br><span style="font-size:.9rem">[Halacha 5 is included in Hilchos Bais HaKnesses 5.]</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 1 — Hands as <i>Remazim</i>: Including Left in Right</div>

<div class="p">For one needs to purify the <span class="kc">hands specifically</span> before eating. For all of man's service in this world is <span class="h">l'achlela smola bimina</span> — to include the left within the right — as is understood from the Holy Zohar in many places. For the entire hold of the <span class="h">sitra achra</span> is from the <span class="h">sitra dismola</span> [side of the left]. And the primary service for which man came into this world is to subdue the <span class="h">sitra achra</span> until the left is included within the right.</div>

<div class="p">And right and left are the aspect of the <span class="kc">upper worlds and lower worlds</span>, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"My hand also laid the foundation of the earth, and My right hand spread out the heavens"</span> <span class="sr">(Isaiah 48:13)</span>. Earth and heavens are the aspect of the lower and upper worlds. And this is the aspect of body and soul, <span class="h">chomer</span> [matter] and <span class="h">tzurah</span> [form]. And this is the aspect of this world and the World to Come. For it is all one concept: the body relative to the soul is in the aspect of this world, matter, the lower worlds, the left. And the soul is in the aspect of the World to Come, form, the upper worlds, the right.</div>

<div class="p">And the main thing is that all these aspects — which are the entirety of the worlds, the upper within the lower and the lower within the upper — should be <span class="kc">included together</span>, as is explained in the Torah teaching mentioned above. And through <span class="kc">eating</span>, the soul is joined with the body. For eating gives life to a person — the soul gives life to the body through eating. For so Hashem, blessed be He, created in His will: that specifically through eating, the vitality of the soul should be drawn to the body. When the soul separates from the body, G-d forbid, that is death. Therefore, Adam HaRishon's first commandment concerned eating. For his primary rectification depended on eating.</div>

<div class="p">And Hashem, blessed be He, desired that all his eating should be only from the <span class="kc">illumination of the <span class="h">Ratzon</span></span>, drawn from <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span> — from which the primary root of livelihood and holy eating [derives]. Through this, the primary completeness of the joining of upper and lower worlds is accomplished — the comprehensions of <span class="h">Ayeh</span> [Where?] and <span class="h">Melo</span> [Fullness], as explained in the Torah teaching. Through this, the primary inclusiveness of left within right. And there is the root of the <span class="kc">two hands</span> — through which heaven and earth were created. They are the aspect of the <span class="h">remazim</span> [hints] in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span> — the contemplation inherent in the holy study that <span class="kc">cannot be explained by mouth explicitly</span>; one completes them with the hands, which are the aspect of <span class="h">remazim</span>.</div>

<div class="p">And through this specifically, the comprehensions of the son and the student — <span class="h">Ayeh</span> and <span class="h">Melo</span> — are included together. For the comprehension of G-dliness is exceedingly exalted — <span class="mx">"no thought can grasp Him at all"</span>; only each one according to what he estimates in his heart. And the main thing is the inclusiveness of <span class="h">Ayeh</span> and <span class="h">Melo</span> — which cannot be grasped in the mind and heart except through the aspect of <span class="h">remazim</span>, the aspect of <span class="kc">hands in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span></span>. From there descend all the holy studies and all contemplation in Torah and <span class="h">yirah</span> — all of which it is impossible to understand from one's <span class="h">rav</span> in completeness except through <span class="h">remazim</span>, which are the aspect of hands.</div>

<div class="p">For even in the wisdoms of Torah in the revealed dimension — in <span class="h">p'shatim</span> in Gemara, the commentary of Tosafos — it is impossible to understand the essence and the sweetness of the <span class="h">p'shat</span> and the <span class="h">chidush</span> except through the <span class="h">rav</span> completing [the explanation] for the student <span class="kc">with his hands</span>, which are <span class="h">remazim</span>. As we see with our senses: the <span class="h">rav</span> explains the <span class="h">p'shat</span> to his students with the movements of his hands. How much more so in the pathways and counsels of the service of Hashem — where the Adversary attacks a person with schemes without number, every day, nearly every hour and moment — such that it is impossible to withstand except through the Torah we received from our <span class="h">Rabbainu</span>, of blessed memory, and the main thing is the <span class="h">remazim</span> within the Torah.</div>

<div class="p">And therefore, one needs to search and seek greatly for a <span class="kc">true <span class="h">Rebbe</span></span> who received from a true <span class="h">Rebbe</span>, and to speak with him time after time — in order to understand the <span class="h">remazim</span> that he hints with his hands: how far to strengthen oneself, etc. etc. And all of this is drawn through holy eating, drawn from the hands that are the <span class="h">remazim</span> in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>, where the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> shines. And therefore, one needs to purify the hands specifically before eating — the purification through water drawn from <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>. And then one merits that eating in holiness has the power to join soul with body — the inclusiveness of upper and lower worlds, the inclusiveness of left within right, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 2 — Morning Hand-Washing</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="kc"><span class="h">netilas yadayim</span> in the morning</span> when one rises from sleep. For sleep is one-sixtieth of death, and the awakening from sleep is the aspect of the resurrection of the dead — as we recite: <span class="v">"Who returns souls to dead bodies."</span> Therefore one needs to purify the hands with the water mentioned above when rising from sleep — which is the aspect of death, the separation of soul from body, from which the <span class="h">sitra achra</span> takes hold. And therefore, during sleep, the <span class="h">sitra achra</span> rests upon his <span class="kc">hands specifically</span>. And when one rises and the soul returns and joins with the body — which is the inclusiveness of upper and lower worlds, the primary completeness — therefore one needs to purify the hands with water drawn from the aspect of <span class="v">"this great and wide sea"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 104:25)</span>. For there is the primary inclusiveness of the worlds — the aspect of the two hands, right and left, included together, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 3 — Purim: Subduing Haman-Amalek</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="kc">Purim</span> — to subdue the <span class="h">klipah</span> of Haman-Amalek, which is the aspect of heresies, as is brought in the words of our Sages, of blessed memory. And the primary heresies come through <span class="kc">questions</span> — when one wants to understand through intellect the inclusiveness of this world within the World to Come, how they are included through our holy Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span>. This is impossible to understand with human intellect in any way — as is understood from the holy <span class="h">sichah</span> printed in <span class="h">Sipurai Ma'asiyos</span> regarding the <span class="h">dreidel</span>, regarding the turning wheel, etc., whose essence was in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, etc. — see there.</div>

<div class="p">And therefore, the miracle came through Mordechai, who took to heart to observe the <span class="h">remazim</span> that Hashem, blessed be He, hints to us at every moment. And the entire malicious intention of Achashvairosh and Haman was to abolish the service of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> — for the primary inclusiveness of the upper within the lower was in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> through the sacrifices. Therefore they were envious and provoked against the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> exceedingly.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 4 — The Purim Meal and <i>Mishloach Manos</i></div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the <span class="kc">Purim feast and <span class="h">mishloach manos</span></span>. For now the eating is in holiness, in the aspect of the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> — drawn from the <span class="h">remazim</span> mentioned above. And therefore, each person sends to his friend <span class="h">manos</span> from the Purim meal — in order to <span class="kc">hint to him</span> the <span class="h">remazim</span> in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>. The primary completeness of the illumination of <span class="h">da'as</span> is through the <span class="h">remazim</span> — from which the holy livelihood of Israel [derives], which is the aspect of the <span class="h">man</span> [manna], the aspect of <span class="mx">"the Torah was only given to the eaters of the manna"</span>, of which it is said: <span class="v">"for they did not know what [<span class="h">mah</span>] it was"</span> <span class="sr">(Exodus 16:15)</span>. The aspect of <span class="mx">"the ultimate knowledge is that we do not know"</span>; only through <span class="h">remazim</span> can one hint to each person according to his level this ultimate knowledge — which is the comprehension of Moshe, the aspect of the true <span class="h">Rebbe</span>, the aspect of the manna that descended in his merit. And this is the aspect of <span class="v">"mishloach manos ish l'rai'aihu"</span> <span class="sr">(Esther 9:22)</span> — food portions in which the <span class="h">remazim</span> are clothed, so that each illuminates his friend the holy <span class="h">da'as</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 5 — Moshe's Hands in the Battle Against Amalek</div>

<div class="p">And therefore, all the subjugation and downfall of Amalek was through the <span class="kc">hands of the <span class="h">Rav</span></span> — the aspect of Moshe — which are <span class="h">remazim</span>, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"And it was, when Moshe raised his hand, Israel prevailed"</span> <span class="sr">(Exodus 17:11)</span>. Our Sages expounded: as long as Israel looked upward, etc. Meaning: Moshe waged war against Amalek — the heresies and questions of the investigators — through the <span class="kc">raising of his hands specifically</span>, which are the <span class="h">remazim</span>: he hinted to Israel with his holy hands that they should look upward, remember all the awesome miracles Hashem did with them, and all the flashes of G-dliness already implanted in their hearts — and not look at the questions and investigations and heresies that [the Adversary] wants to introduce into the heart. For time does not suffice to answer them. And therefore, <span class="mx">"a fence for wisdom is silence"</span> — as explained there. The <span class="h">Rav</span> needs at times to be silent, and the students who truly desire will understand many <span class="h">remazim</span> through his silence and his holy movements, which are the aspect of hands.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 6 — Purim, <i>Parashas Parah</i>, and the Dispute of Korach</div>

<div class="p">And through this, you will understand some hints regarding what is written (<span class="lr">LM II, 74</span>): after Purim we read <span class="h">Parashas Parah</span> — for initially it is <span class="h">pur</span> [lot], and afterward it becomes <span class="h">parah</span> [heifer]. Also, we heard from his holy mouth that on Purim he was engaged in <span class="kc">rectifying the sin of the dispute of Korach</span>, and he said it is an exceedingly deep secret. And the hint is in the verse: <span class="v">"And if from the front of his face his head becomes bald"</span> <span class="sr">(Leviticus 13:41)</span> — the initial letters spell <span class="h">Purim Korach</span>, etc. See there in the <span class="h">Sichos</span>.</div>

<div class="p">And the matter is somewhat understood from afar according to what is found in the Pesikta (brought in <span class="h">Yalkut Reuveni</span> on <span class="h">Parashas Chukas</span>): "Why is <span class="h">Parashas Parah</span> said after the dispute of Korach? Because the Kehathites were purified to carry the Ark of the Covenant of Hashem, as it is said: <span class="v">'On the shoulder they shall carry'</span> <span class="sr">(Numbers 7:9)</span>. Korach said: 'It is not enough that they removed me from the <span class="h">Kehunah</span> and the gifts — but he shaved my head and made me a fool.' Immediately he became envious of Moshe and challenged him. And he did not know: the statute of the <span class="h">Parah Adumah</span> [Red Heifer] shall be an atonement for Israel." Thus far the Pesikta. And it is a wonder: what does the statute of the <span class="h">Parah Adumah</span> have to do with the dispute of Korach? But according to the words of <span class="h">Rabbainu</span>, of blessed memory — who hinted to us that they are connected: on Purim he rectified the dispute of Korach, and from Purim is made the aspect of the <span class="h">Parah Adumah</span> — it emerges that he explained to us that <span class="kc">the <span class="h">Parah Adumah</span> is the rectification of the dispute of Korach</span>. And this is the matter of the Pesikta mentioned above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 7 — The Secret of the <i>Parah Adumah</i> and Including Left in Right</div>

<div class="p">But the secret of the matter is certainly impossible to understand. For it is the secret of the <span class="h">Parah Adumah</span>, which is a hidden and deeply sealed statute, about which it is said: <span class="v">"I said I would become wise, but it is far from me"</span> <span class="sr">(Ecclesiastes 7:23)</span>. Nevertheless, whatever hints we can find in this, we are obligated to strive and find. For even in all the holy books, they did not explain to us this secret of the <span class="h">Parah</span>. Yet they still revealed to us many hints within this hidden secret.</div>

<div class="p">For it is already explained that the primary <span class="h">tikkun</span> for which man was created is to include left within right, etc. And this was the entire blemish of Korach — as it is found in the <span class="h">Tikunim</span> and in the Holy Zohar: <span class="mx">"Korach sought to exchange left for right"</span> <span class="hd">[<span class="h">d'Korach ba'i l'achlafa smola bimina</span>]</span>. For Korach was a Levi — the aspect of the left in holiness, as is known. But from there [comes] the entire hold of the chain of harsh <span class="h">dinim</span> [judgments]. And his entire service should have been to sweeten them by having the left included within [the right] — through Moshe and Aharon, who transferred to the Levi'im the carrying of the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>. In particular, Korach, who was one of the bearers of the Ark, needed to draw upon himself the holiness of the Torah in the Ark.</div>

<div class="p">And the primary holiness was that he needed to <span class="kc">nullify his <span class="h">da'as</span> and his wisdoms</span> before Moshe — for this is the primary wisdom: to nullify one's <span class="h">da'as</span> completely, especially before such a true <span class="h">Rebbe</span> as Moshe Rabbainu, peace be upon him. And Korach blemished this greatly and relied on his own <span class="h">da'as</span> and disputed Moshe. And through this, he separated left from right, until the severe <span class="h">dinim</span> intensified against him and he fell to the lowest depths of <span class="h">She'ol</span>, may the Merciful One save us.</div>

<div class="p">And therefore, his blemish was that he complained that [Moshe] had made him a fool by shaving his hair. And he wanted to be wise. And this was his entire blemish, as mentioned above. And therefore he blemished through protesting the shaving of his hair. For the hairs are <span class="h">mosrai mochin</span> [excess aspects of the mind], and in holiness they are very exalted — for through them the <span class="h">Rebbe</span> illuminates contemplation to his students, etc. But when they proliferate excessively, especially when there are blemishes, the <span class="h">dinim</span> take hold of them and they need to be shaved. (As explained regarding the prohibition of theft, <span class="lr">LM I, 69</span>, and see what is explained there on the verse <span class="v">"the heart of the wise is to his right, and the heart of the fool is to his left"</span> <span class="sr">(Ecclesiastes 10:2)</span> — see there.)</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the mitzvah of shaving the hair of the <span class="h">metzora</span> [leper], which comes through <span class="h">machlokes</span> — through speaking <span class="h">lashon hara</span> — which is the aspect of the sin of Korach. And therefore, the Levi'im needed a shaving like that of the <span class="h">metzora</span> and the impure, as Rashi explains there. For they are from the left side, where the <span class="h">dinim</span> take hold, primarily in the hairs, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">But all of this is impossible to understand — for the inclusiveness of left within right cannot be grasped in completeness. And therefore, <span class="mx">"a fence for wisdom is silence"</span> — which is itself the <span class="h">remazim</span> mentioned above in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>. For there are many things that one does not understand except through the aspect of silence — which is the aspect of <span class="h">remazim</span>, the aspect of the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>.</div>

<div class="p">And the primary contemplation that one understands through the revelation of the Torah of the true <span class="h">Rebbe</span> — is what one understands from him, each according to his level: that up to here is <span class="h">t'chum Shabbos</span> [the Shabbos boundary], and it is impossible to understand further — as is explained there in the commentary regarding the <span class="h">makifin</span> [surrounding lights], etc. For this reason one needs to be silent, so as not to enter into questions and answers that are beyond time, etc. It emerges that the primary thing the <span class="h">Rav</span> communicates to each person, illuminating <span class="h">da'as</span> in him to save him from transgressions — the main thing is that he explains to him that he should understand from afar that there are true wisdoms and wondrous comprehensions in the Torah — so that his <span class="h">emunah</span> should be strengthened, until he can come to the aspect of <span class="h">bittul</span> [self-nullification] toward the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, the aspect of the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>. But the moment one wants to understand and bring it into one's <span class="h">da'as</span>, he blemishes this, and needs to flee from this greatly — in the aspect of: <span class="v">"Into that which is too wondrous for you, do not investigate"</span>, etc.</div>

<div class="p">And all of this is what <span class="h">Rabbainu</span>, of blessed memory, explained (in the Torah teaching <span class="v">"Chadi Rabbi Shimon"</span>, <span class="lr">LM I, 61</span>) on the verse <span class="v">"for it is your wisdom and understanding in the eyes of the nations"</span> <span class="sr">(Deuteronomy 4:6)</span> etc. — regarding the secret of the intercalation [<span class="h">sod ha'ibur</span>]: that there is a wisdom in this that can be revealed, and yet they will not know the secret — they will only understand that the secret is with us, etc. See there. And the same applies among Israel themselves, between the <span class="h">Rav</span> and the students and the rest of the world: they reveal wisdoms and exceedingly deep and exalted counsels, yet the secret remains with them. Only through their revelation, they make it understood to the world that the secret remains with them.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the <span class="kc"><span class="h">Parah Adumah</span>, whose procedures are performed outside</span> [the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>]. And it is a statute, hidden and sealed more than all the <span class="h">mitzvos</span>. For in the sacrifices that were in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, through them the worlds were included — upper below and lower above — as explained regarding the wheel [the <span class="h">dreidel</span>], etc. And even though that secret too cannot be grasped, nevertheless — since the service of the sacrifices was in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, where all the Torah and all comprehensions were — one can speak about it. But the <span class="h">Parah</span> — her procedures are literally outside, and she is a wondrous statute. Her entire service was that he slaughtered her facing the entrance of the <span class="h">Ohel Mo'ed</span>: he slaughtered her outside and gazed toward the <span class="h">Kodesh HaKodashim</span>. This is the aspect of <span class="v">"in the eyes of the nations"</span> mentioned above — that from outside, one gazes inside at the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, which is the aspect of the Torah that stood there, the aspect of the true <span class="h">Rebbe</span>, the true <span class="h">da'as</span>, the aspect of Moshe, the aspect of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> — for <span class="mx">"whoever has <span class="h">da'as</span>, it is as if the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> was built in his days."</span></div>

<div class="p">And one needs to gaze toward there. But the main thing — one needs to know that he is very, very far from this <span class="h">da'as</span>, and it is forbidden to enter into it by wanting, G-d forbid, to understand the questions that are hidden from him, each according to his level. For about this it is said: <span class="v">"I said I would become wise, but it is far from me."</span> And therefore the <span class="h">Parah</span> purifies from the impurity of the dead — which Adam HaRishon caused by eating from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil. For the primary blemish was that he gazed into that which he had no permission to gaze — for he wanted to know good and evil, which derive from right and left, to know through intellect all their root and how they are included together, etc., as mentioned above. And through this, death was decreed — which is separation, as mentioned above. And therefore, through the <span class="h">Parah</span> — which is slaughtered facing the entrance of the <span class="h">Ohel Mo'ed</span>, which is the aspect mentioned above of gazing only from afar at the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> — through this, the blemish of the impurity of the dead (caused by Adam HaRishon who blemished this) is purified and rectified. And all of this is the rectification of the sin of Korach, who blemished this, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And all of this is what we work to rectify on Purim, as mentioned above. For Haman cast the <span class="h">pur</span> [lot] and wanted to blemish the <span class="h">goral</span> [lot] of Yom Kippur that the <span class="h">Kohen</span> placed upon the two <span class="h">se'irim</span> [goats] — one for Hashem and one for <span class="h">Azazel</span> — which is the secret of the <span class="h">Parah Adumah</span> whose procedures are outside, as is brought. And the two <span class="h">se'irim</span> are the aspect of <span class="h">se'aros</span> [hairs] mentioned above, the aspect of <span class="v">"Behold, Eisav my brother is a hairy man"</span> <span class="sr">(Genesis 27:11)</span> who was full of hair, etc. But within the hairs themselves — which are the aspect of the two <span class="h">se'irim</span> — there are two aspects, as mentioned above: the aspect of <span class="v">"the heart of the wise is to his right"</span> etc. And this is impossible to grasp. Therefore one needs to cast a lot [to determine] which is for Hashem, etc. And Haman denied this, and said he would draw the lot through his sorcery as he wished, to overpower Mordechai and all Israel, G-d forbid. And Hashem, blessed be He, in His mercy, reversed the matter until they hanged him and his offspring, etc. And through this, Purim was made — named after the <span class="h">pur</span> [lot] — until from this was made the aspect of the <span class="h">Parah Adumah</span>, whose procedures are outside and one gazes inward, etc. — which is the primary aspect of <span class="v">"I said I would become wise, but it is far from me"</span> — through which the primary rectification [is accomplished], as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 8 — <i>Parashas HaChodesh</i> and the Secret of Intercalation</div>

<div class="p">And therefore, afterward we immediately read <span class="kc"><span class="h">Parashas HaChodesh</span></span> — which is the secret of the sanctification of the new month, the secret of the intercalation. For all of this is the aspect of the secret of the intercalation, the aspect of <span class="v">"for it is your wisdom and understanding in the eyes of the nations"</span> — <span class="v">"in the eyes of the nations"</span> specifically, as mentioned above (see there in the Torah teaching <span class="v">"Chadi Rabbi Shimon"</span> mentioned above, <span class="lr">LM I, 61</span>, and you will understand). For the primary secret of the intercalation — the secret of the sanctification of the new month, which Moshe found difficult — is to rectify the blemish of the moon, until the light of the moon will be like the light of the sun. Which is the aspect of the inclusiveness of the <span class="h">aspaklaria she'ainah me'irah</span> [the non-luminous looking glass], which is the aspect of the moon, within the aspect of the <span class="h">aspaklaria hame'irah</span> [the luminous looking glass], which is the aspect of the sun. Which is the aspect of the inclusiveness of the comprehension of the lower worlds — <span class="v">"the fullness of the whole earth is His glory"</span> — within the comprehension of the upper worlds — the aspect of <span class="h">Ayeh</span> — as is explained there in the Torah teaching <span class="v">"Ki Merachamam"</span> mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">For all of this is impossible to include in completeness except through the aspect of silence, the aspect of <span class="h">remazim</span>, the aspect of the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, etc. — which is the primary rectification for all the above. And in all of this we are engaged before Pesach — when the primary beginning of Israel's drawing close to their Father in Heaven [takes place], through Moshe Rabbainu who illuminates <span class="h">da'as</span> in all of Israel, etc., and the primary rectification is through this, as mentioned above. See further regarding the main point in another place, in Hilchos Peter Chamor, where the matter of Pesach and <span class="h">matzah</span> and the prohibition of <span class="h">chametz</span> are explained according to the Torah teaching <span class="v">"Ki Merachamam"</span> mentioned above. But here, many things have been added regarding the Four <span class="h">Parshos</span>. And still the secret remains sealed and hidden from us — only we see from afar, etc. And there is yet more to say to G-d.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 9 — The Practical Lesson: Beware of Self-Wisdom</div>

<div class="p">And the main thing for practical application — whatever a person's situation may be — <span class="kc">he needs to be careful, at the very least, against <span class="h">chochmos</span> [self-wisdom]</span>. That he should not be wise in his own eyes. For the <span class="h">chochmos</span> of the world are very harmful to every single person, as <span class="h">Rabbainu</span>, of blessed memory, wrote: <span class="mx">"<span class="h">Chochmos</span> cause a person to fall."</span> And as it is written: <span class="v">"Have you seen a man wise in his own eyes? There is more hope for a fool than for him"</span> <span class="sr">(Proverbs 26:12)</span>. And especially when one comes through his <span class="h">chochmos</span> to <span class="h">machlokes</span> [dispute], G-d forbid — all the more so when one disputes through this those who are more upright than him, etc.</div>

<div class="p">And there are those who say with a full mouth in truth that so-and-so is G-d-fearing and more upright than him, yet nevertheless, he does not conduct himself properly — because it seems to him that he is wise in his own eyes, as is very commonly found in many forms. And this is more harmful to every person than all the transgressions. For <span class="kc"><span class="h">machlokes</span> and <span class="h">sinas chinam</span></span> [baseless hatred] are worse than everything — and through them the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> was destroyed, as our Sages, of blessed memory, said. And still the Satan dances to prevent the building of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> through this — through the abundance of <span class="h">machlokes</span> and accusation in the entire world, especially among the scholars and the upright, etc.</div>

<div class="p">For as long as a person guards himself from every type of <span class="h">machlokes</span> in truth, and nullifies his <span class="h">da'as</span> before the <span class="h">da'as</span> of the Torah and the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — there is <span class="kc">hope for him always</span>, whatever his situation may be. For <span class="maxim">"great is Hashem, and we do not know at all"</span>, etc. — as this holy conversation is recorded in <span class="h">Chayai Mohara"n</span>. And it is understood there that the primary hope and rectification is through believing in the greatness of Hashem and His Torah and His <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, and [recognizing] that we do not know at all, etc.</div>

<!-- Translator's Summary -->
<div class="ts">
  <div class="sh">✦ Translator's Summary ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>Halacha 6</strong> develops an entirely new dimension of hand-washing: the hands as <em>remazim</em> — gestures, hints, the non-verbal dimension of Torah transmission that operates above <em>da'as</em>, in the realm of <em>Ratzon</em>.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>The core teaching:</strong> Man's entire service is to include left within right — body within soul, this world within the World to Come, earth within heaven. This is accomplished through <em>eating</em> (which binds soul to body) — but only when eating is drawn from the <em>Ratzon</em> level, which flows through the "hands in the Sea of Wisdom." These "hands" are the <em>remazim</em> — hints and gestures that convey what explicit speech cannot. Even in nigleh (Gemara, Tosafos), the rav completes his teaching with hand-movements.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>Purim:</strong> Amalek = heresies that come from trying to intellectually grasp left-right inclusiveness. The antidote is Mordechai observing the <em>remazim</em> that Hashem hints at every moment. <em>Mishloach Manos</em> = sending food portions that carry <em>remazim</em> of the manna-level <em>da'as</em>. Moshe's raised hands against Amalek = <em>remazim</em> that tell Israel to look upward rather than at the questions.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>Korach and <em>Parah Adumah</em>:</strong> Korach wanted to "exchange left for right" — relying on his own <em>da'as</em> rather than nullifying before Moshe. The <em>Parah Adumah</em>, performed <em>outside</em> while gazing <em>inward</em> toward the <em>Kodesh HaKodashim</em>, embodies the principle: see from afar that the secret exists, but don't try to enter it. "I said I would become wise, but it is far from me." Purim → <em>Parah</em> → <em>Parashas HaChodesh</em> → Pesach: the sequence of the Four <em>Parshos</em> is a progressive rectification of this same principle.</p>

  <p><strong>The practical lesson (§9):</strong> Guard against <em>chochmos</em> (self-wisdom) above all. Nullify your <em>da'as</em> before the true <em>tzadikim</em>. Avoid <em>machlokes</em> at all costs. As long as one does this — there is hope always, whatever one's situation. <em>"Great is Hashem, and we do not know at all."</em></p>
